        ("Fundamental of Electronics and Engineering", "Which type of Analog-to-Digital Converter (ADC) is known for having the fastest conversion time?", "Successive Approximation Register (SAR) ADC", "Dual-Slope Integrating ADC", "Flash ADC", "Delta-Sigma ADC", "Flash ADC"),
        ("Fundamental of Electronics and Engineering", "In a Phase-Locked Loop (PLL), the relationship between the 'Lock Range' and the 'Capture Range' is generally:", "Capture Range is always equal to Lock Range.", "Capture Range is strictly greater than Lock Range.", "Capture Range is strictly less than or equal to the Lock Range.", "They are entirely independent of each other.", "Capture Range is strictly less than or equal to the Lock Range."),
        ("Fundamental of Electronics and Engineering", "In a Superheterodyne receiver, if the intermediate frequency (IF) is 455 kHz and the tuned station is at 1000 kHz, what is the image frequency (assuming the local oscillator is tracked above the RF)?", "1455 kHz", "1910 kHz", "545 kHz", "455 kHz", "1910 kHz"),
        ("Fundamental of Electronics and Engineering", "What is the primary disadvantage of a Class B push-pull power amplifier?", "Extremely low theoretical efficiency.", "Severe cross-over distortion as the signal crosses zero volts.", "Inability to amplify AC signals.", "Excessive static power dissipation when no signal is applied.", "Severe cross-over distortion as the signal crosses zero volts."),
        ("Fundamental of Electronics and Engineering", "A Wien Bridge oscillator is typically used for generating audio frequencies. Its frequency of oscillation is given by:", "f = 1 / (2 * pi * sqrt(RC))", "f = 1 / (2 * pi * R * C)", "f = 1 / (2 * pi * sqrt(LC))", "f = R / (2 * pi * L)", "f = 1 / (2 * pi * R * C)"),
        ("Fundamental of Electronics and Engineering", "When a 555 Timer IC is configured in astable multivibrator mode using standard resistors Ra and Rb, the duty cycle of the output wave can never be:", "Exactly 50% or lower", "Higher than 50%", "Higher than 90%", "Between 50% and 99%", "Exactly 50% or lower"),
        ("Fundamental of Electronics and Engineering", "The Hall Effect can be used to determine whether a semiconductor is N-type or P-type by measuring the:", "Resistivity of the sample.", "Polarity of the induced Hall voltage.", "Thermal conductivity of the sample.", "Carrier lifetime.", "Polarity of the induced Hall voltage."),
        ("Fundamental of Electronics and Engineering", "A Varactor diode acts as a voltage-controlled capacitor. Its capacitance is formed by the:", "Diffusion capacitance of a forward-biased junction.", "Depletion region capacitance of a reverse-biased PN junction.", "Dielectric layer between the metal and semiconductor.", "Parasitic package capacitance.", "Depletion region capacitance of a reverse-biased PN junction."),
        ("Fundamental of Electronics and Engineering", "Why does a Schottky diode switch significantly faster than a standard PN junction diode?", "It has a larger bandgap.", "It operates using majority carriers only, so there is no minority carrier charge storage to deplete.", "It has a heavily doped P-region.", "It operates at higher voltages.", "It operates using majority carriers only, so there is no minority carrier charge storage to deplete."),
        ("Fundamental of Electronics and Engineering", "The 'Body Effect' in a MOSFET causes the threshold voltage (Vt) to:", "Increase when the source-to-body voltage (Vsb) increases.", "Decrease when the source-to-body voltage (Vsb) increases.", "Remain completely unaffected by Vsb.", "Become negative for an enhancement-type device.", "Increase when the source-to-body voltage (Vsb) increases."),
        ("Fundamental of Electronics and Engineering", "What is a defining characteristic of an Instrumentation Amplifier compared to a standard difference amplifier?", "Lower gain.", "Extremely high input impedance on both input terminals.", "Single-ended input.", "Lack of Common-Mode Rejection.", "Extremely high input impedance on both input terminals."),
        ("Fundamental of Electronics and Engineering", "Which combinational logic device is often referred to as a 'Universal Logic Function Generator' because it can implement any Boolean function of N variables?", "A 2-to-4 Decoder", "An N-bit Adder", "A 2^N-to-1 Multiplexer", "A magnitude comparator", "A 2^N-to-1 Multiplexer"),
        ("Fundamental of Electronics and Engineering", "In digital synchronous design, violating the 'setup time' or 'hold time' of a flip-flop leads to an unpredictable output state known as:", "Race condition", "Metastability", "Ground bounce", "Clock skew", "Metastability"),
        ("Fundamental of Electronics and Engineering", "According to the Shannon-Hartley theorem, the channel capacity (C) of a communication channel is fundamentally limited by:", "Only the bandwidth of the channel.", "Only the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR).", "The bandwidth of the channel and the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR).", "The speed of light.", "The bandwidth of the channel and the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR)."),
        ("Fundamental of Electronics and Engineering", "For a bandpass signal with frequency components strictly between 10 MHz and 12 MHz, what is the theoretical minimum (Nyquist) sampling rate required to avoid aliasing?", "24 MHz", "20 MHz", "4 MHz", "2 MHz", "4 MHz"),
        ("Fundamental of Electronics and Engineering", "In Pulse Code Modulation (PCM), 'quantization noise' can be reduced by:", "Decreasing the sampling rate.", "Increasing the number of quantization levels (bits per sample).", "Using an analog filter at the receiver.", "Transmitting in half-duplex mode.", "Increasing the number of quantization levels (bits per sample)."),
        ("Fundamental of Electronics and Engineering", "In the context of linear voltage regulators (like the 7805), what does 'Dropout Voltage' mean?", "The minimum voltage required at the input to keep the output in regulation.", "The voltage at which the device burns out.", "The difference between the input voltage and the output voltage where regulation fails.", "The ripple voltage on the output.", "The difference between the input voltage and the output voltage where regulation fails."),
        ("Fundamental of Electronics and Engineering", "For a Silicon Controlled Rectifier (SCR) to turn off (commutate), the anode current must drop below a specific threshold known as the:", "Latching current", "Holding current", "Gate trigger current", "Reverse leakage current", "Holding current"),
        ("Fundamental of Electronics and Engineering", "A TRIAC is essentially equivalent to:", "Two SCRs connected in anti-parallel with a common gate.", "Two diodes connected in series.", "A BJT and a FET connected in cascade.", "A single PNPN structure with no gate.", "Two SCRs connected in anti-parallel with a common gate."),
        ("Fundamental of Electronics and Engineering", "The emission wavelength of a Light Emitting Diode (LED) is fundamentally determined by:", "The forward current driven through it.", "The physical size of the diode package.", "The bandgap energy of the semiconductor material used.", "The doping concentration of the N-region only.", "The bandgap energy of the semiconductor material used."),
        ("Fundamental of Electronics and Engineering", "In a common-base BJT amplifier configuration, what are the typical characteristics regarding current gain (Ai) and voltage gain (Av)?", "High Ai, Low Av", "Low Ai (approx 1), High Av", "High Ai, High Av", "Low Ai, Low Av", "Low Ai (approx 1), High Av"),
        ("Fundamental of Electronics and Engineering", "In an ideal transformer, the impedance matching property states that a load impedance Z_L connected to the secondary reflects to the primary as:", "Z_L * (N1/N2)", "Z_L * (N2/N1)", "Z_L * (N1/N2)^2", "Z_L / (N1/N2)^2", "Z_L * (N1/N2)^2"),
        ("Fundamental of Electronics and Engineering", "In FM (Frequency Modulation) communication, what is the 'Capture Effect'?", "The ability of the receiver to completely suppress a weaker interfering signal on the same frequency in favor of a stronger one.", "The locking of the local oscillator to the carrier frequency.", "The capturing of noise spikes by the discriminator.", "The attenuation of high frequencies in the audio signal.", "The ability of the receiver to completely suppress a weaker interfering signal on the same frequency in favor of a stronger one."),
            
            # --- Fundamental of Mechanical Engineering ---
