        ("Fundamental of Electrical Engineering", "An autotransformer offers a significant saving in copper compared to a two-winding transformer primarily when:", "The transformation ratio (K) is very high (e.g., K > 10)", "The transformation ratio (K) is approximately equal to 1", "It is used for stepping up voltage", "It is used in high-frequency applications", "The transformation ratio (K) is approximately equal to 1"),
        ("Fundamental of Electrical Engineering", "The 'All-Day Efficiency' of a distribution transformer is defined as the ratio of:", "Output power to input power measured at peak load", "Energy output in kWh to energy input in kWh over 24 hours", "Maximum efficiency to full-load efficiency", "Output voltage to input voltage over 24 hours", "Energy output in kWh to energy input in kWh over 24 hours"),
        ("Fundamental of Electrical Engineering", "In a DC generator, the primary function of the commutator is to:", "Reduce the armature reaction", "Provide a smooth DC output by mechanically rectifying the induced alternating EMF", "Increase the magnetic flux density", "Prevent sparking at the brushes", "Provide a smooth DC output by mechanically rectifying the induced alternating EMF"),
        ("Fundamental of Electrical Engineering", "Armature reaction in a DC machine carrying a load typically causes the Main Magnetic Flux to:", "Remain unaffected", "Distort and decrease (demagnetizing effect)", "Increase symmetrically", "Rotate at synchronous speed", "Distort and decrease (demagnetizing effect)"),
        ("Fundamental of Electrical Engineering", "In a DC motor, if the field flux is abruptly reduced to zero while the motor is running under light load, what is the immediate consequence?", "The motor will stop immediately", "The motor speed will decrease proportionally", "The motor will accelerate to a dangerously high speed", "The armature current will drop to zero", "The motor will accelerate to a dangerously high speed"),
        ("Fundamental of Electrical Engineering", "Which of the following methods of speed control for a DC shunt motor provides speeds strictly BELOW the rated base speed?", "Field resistance control (Flux control)", "Armature voltage control", "Tapping the field winding", "Using a diverter resistance across the field", "Armature voltage control"),
        ("Fundamental of Electrical Engineering", "The starting torque of a DC series motor is extremely high because:", "Torque is directly proportional to the square of the armature current (T ∝ Ia^2) before magnetic saturation.", "It has a very low armature resistance.", "The field flux is constant regardless of load.", "It operates on a high voltage supply.", "Torque is directly proportional to the square of the armature current (T ∝ Ia^2) before magnetic saturation."),
        ("Fundamental of Electrical Engineering", "For a 3-phase Induction Motor, the relative speed between the rotating stator magnetic field and the rotor itself is equal to:", "Synchronous speed (Ns)", "Rotor speed (Nr)", "Slip speed (Ns - Nr)", "Zero", "Slip speed (Ns - Nr)"),
        ("Fundamental of Electrical Engineering", "In a 3-phase Induction Motor, maximum torque occurs at a specific slip (s). What is the condition for this maximum torque?", "Slip = 1 (at standstill)", "Rotor resistance equals rotor leakage reactance (R2 = X2)", "Rotor resistance is zero", "Slip = 0 (at synchronous speed)", "Rotor resistance equals rotor leakage reactance (R2 = X2)"),
        ("Fundamental of Electrical Engineering", "The phenomenon of 'Cogging' (magnetic locking) in a squirrel cage induction motor can be minimized by:", "Increasing the air gap", "Skewing the rotor slots", "Increasing the supply voltage", "Adding external resistance to the stator", "Skewing the rotor slots"),
        ("Fundamental of Electrical Engineering", "If a 3-phase induction motor is running at a slip 's', the ratio of Rotor Copper Loss to Rotor Gross Power Input (Air-gap power) is:", "1 - s", "s", "s / (1-s)", "1 / s", "s"),
        ("Fundamental of Electrical Engineering", "A Synchronous Motor operates at a leading power factor when it is:", "Under-excited", "Critically excited", "Over-excited", "Operating at no load with zero excitation", "Over-excited"),
        ("Fundamental of Electrical Engineering", "The 'V-curves' of a synchronous motor plot the relationship between:", "Armature current and Field current", "Power factor and Armature current", "Torque and Speed", "Terminal voltage and Load current", "Armature current and Field current"),
        ("Fundamental of Electrical Engineering", "Why is a standard 3-phase synchronous motor not inherently self-starting?", "Due to its high inertia", "Because the net average torque over one complete electrical cycle is zero", "Because it requires a DC supply for the stator", "Because the air gap is too large", "Because the net average torque over one complete electrical cycle is zero"),
        ("Fundamental of Electrical Engineering", "To make a synchronous motor self-starting, which of the following is commonly provided on the rotor?", "A commutator", "Slip rings", "Damper (Amortisseur) windings", "An extra DC field winding", "Damper (Amortisseur) windings"),
        ("Fundamental of Electrical Engineering", "In a Permanent Magnet Moving Coil (PMMC) instrument, the deflecting torque is directly proportional to:", "The square of the current", "The current", "The voltage only", "The rate of change of current", "The current"),
        ("Fundamental of Electrical Engineering", "Which instrument type can be utilized to measure both AC and DC quantities with the same calibration?", "PMMC (Permanent Magnet Moving Coil)", "Induction type", "Moving Iron (MI) type", "Galvanometer", "Moving Iron (MI) type"),
        ("Fundamental of Electrical Engineering", "To extend the range of a DC ammeter, a purely resistive component is connected in:", "Series with the meter (a Multiplier)", "Parallel with the meter (a Shunt)", "Series with the load", "Parallel with the load", "Parallel with the meter (a Shunt)"),
        ("Fundamental of Electrical Engineering", "In a dynamometer-type wattmeter, the fixed coils are typically connected in:", "Parallel with the load to measure voltage", "Series with the load to carry the load current", "Series with a high resistance", "Parallel with the supply", "Series with the load to carry the load current"),
        ("Fundamental of Electrical Engineering", "The phantom loading test is performed on energy meters primarily to:", "Test the meter at high voltages", "Reduce power consumption during the testing calibration process", "Check for creeping errors", "Test the meter under short-circuit conditions", "Reduce power consumption during the testing calibration process"),
        ("Fundamental of Electrical Engineering", "In a purely capacitive AC circuit, the current wave:", "Lags the voltage wave by 90 degrees", "Leads the voltage wave by 90 degrees", "Is exactly in phase with the voltage", "Leads the voltage wave by 45 degrees", "Leads the voltage wave by 90 degrees"),
        ("Fundamental of Electrical Engineering", "The Power Factor of an AC circuit is defined as the ratio of:", "Apparent power to Active power", "Reactive power to Active power", "Active power to Apparent power", "Reactive power to Apparent power", "Active power to Apparent power"),
        ("Fundamental of Electrical Engineering", "In a star-connected (Y) 3-phase system, the relationship between line voltage (V_L) and phase voltage (V_ph) is:", "V_L = V_ph", "V_L = sqrt(3) * V_ph", "V_L = V_ph / sqrt(3)", "V_L = 3 * V_ph", "V_L = sqrt(3) * V_ph"),
        ("Fundamental of Electrical Engineering", "In a delta-connected (Δ) 3-phase system, the line current (I_L) is related to the phase current (I_ph) by:", "I_L = I_ph", "I_L = sqrt(3) * I_ph", "I_L = I_ph / sqrt(3)", "I_L = 3 * I_ph", "I_L = sqrt(3) * I_ph"),
        ("Fundamental of Electrical Engineering", "Which theorem is mathematically the dual of Thevenin's Theorem?", "Superposition Theorem", "Norton's Theorem", "Maximum Power Transfer Theorem", "Tellegen's Theorem", "Norton's Theorem"),
        ("Fundamental of Electrical Engineering", "The form factor of a pure sinusoidal alternating current wave is strictly:", "1.11", "1.414", "0.707", "0.637", "1.11"),
        ("Fundamental of Electrical Engineering", "According to Faraday's Law of Electromagnetic Induction, the magnitude of the induced EMF in a coil is directly proportional to:", "The magnetic flux density", "The rate of change of magnetic flux linkages", "The resistance of the coil", "The cross-sectional area only", "The rate of change of magnetic flux linkages"),
